A||||7||GTMike||can an off alignment cause car to shake?||||1||
Z||000000||GTMike||07-16-2001||11:56 PM||||my car has a little shake going on at around 80mph.   it starts in the stearing wheel and  then it moves back to the rear of the car. its like a wave affect.     i had the wheels balanced today. and it seemed to help a tinny bit. <P>the shop said that the alignment wont  cause the car to shake at all.   <P>the car drives str8. it doesn't wonder at all. and i have no play in the stearing. <BR>it is tight and very responsive.  <BR>could my alignment be off just a little  and caues the shake. <P>it is smooth at all rpms   at slower speeds.<BR>so i dont think its a moter or tranny mount. <P>no creaks grinding or anything abnormal in stearing the car.<P>any suggestions???||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||07-17-2001||12:05 AM||||I would look into the condition of the ball joints and control arm bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Soelas||07-17-2001||01:04 AM||||What are the condition of your tires? Are they rated for the speed?<P>My friend had a 86 Camaro (v6) that he put a 327 into. He always complained of a shimmy at about 140kmh (85mph). He had an alignment done (not sure if he had anything else done to it) to try and correct the problem.<P>To make a long story short. He was using some really cheap tires and the tread was separating more and more everytime he was overdriving the tires. It's a good thing someone pointed this out to him. Blowing out a tire at 85mph might be slightly bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTMike||07-17-2001||02:10 AM||||200 r60 15.  fornt and rear.  but the rear is aobut an inch or a little more shorter than the front. because they are really really warn down. the fronts are very new. <P>edit:  ok i just realized that i'm suposed to have 215s on the rear of my car.   it looks like the last owner took the front old front tires and put them on the back and put new ones on the front.   <P>cold fairly bald tires on the back of the wrong size  cause any viberation or shaking?<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by GTMike (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||07-17-2001||06:22 AM||||It could be a number of things but saying that alignment can't cause shake is wrong.<BR>Look for uneven treadware.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||07-17-2001||09:04 AM||||Try finding a shop that can test the balance under load. While a tire may balance just going around once you put weight of a car on it, all kinds of imperfections invisible to the naked eye become apparent. It sounds like the previous owner was a cheap bastard, and probably put the cheapest Pep Boys rejects on it. My money is on the balance of the tires. Save your $ and get 4 new tires. It's the most important part on your car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||gtcash||07-17-2001||09:43 AM||||Ply seperation! That's what I put my money on. Been there, done that...starts out a small vibration, and gradually builds to a real shaker! Hang on, you're in for a ride of you life!!!<BR>"The Mayor"||||reg||6
Z||000007||rogergarrison||07-17-2001||12:23 PM||||alignment usually just causes weird tire wear, poor handling. Most times shaking is from wheels/tires. Ive had lots of problems buying new tires that are out of round. I always hang around and watch the runnout while there being balanced. a tire wobbling on a balancer (even if it is balanced) will still shake your car.||||reg||1
